  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 PM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nThis Afternoon and Evening \n\nWe are already seeing rapid recovery taking place in the wake of\nthe mesoscale convective complex across north-central Iowa and\nsouth-central Minnesota. Temperatures are currently ranging from\nthe mid-80s to lower 90s. Dew points are in the lower and\nmid-70s. This is resulting in surface-based CAPES of 3-4K. As\nthe afternoon progresses, this air mass will spread into the\nUpper Mississippi River Valley ahead of a cold front extending\nfrom the Minnesota Arrowhead to the southwest tip of Minnesota.\nThere is already scattered showers and storms along this front.\nThese storms should impact the Upper Mississippi River Valley\nbetween 3 pm and 9 pm.\n\nFurther south, moderate to strong capping (10-12C 700 mb \ntemperatures) has developed across western Iowa and far southern\nMinnesota. This should keep this sector relatively clean; thus,\nthis instability reservoir should continue to be able feed\nstorms this afternoon and early evening.\n\nAs a vigorous shortwave moves into the region this afternoon,\nthe winds aloft will strengthen. This will result 0-6 km shear\nof 60-65 knots. As a result, initial storms will be likely\nsupercells. The hodographs are exceptionally long from eastern \nportions or northeast Iowa northeast through central Wisconsin. \nIf these storms can become orthogonal with the cold front, \nthere could be a few tornadoes. SigTOR parameter is 8 in \nsouthwest Wisconsin. \n\nIn addition with large NCAPE and strong mid-level lapse, there \ncould be some large hail with the earlier supercells. Over time \nas cold pools coalesce, damaging winds will become the primary \nthreat. In addition, any surges along the line will have to be \nwatched for the potential for a few QLCS tornadoes.\n\nTuesday through Saturday\n\nHigh pressure will build across the area on Tuesday and then\nprovide dry weather through Saturday. High temperatures will be\nprimarily in the 70s and 80s. The coolest day will be on\nWednesday when highs will be in the lower and mid-70s.",
